Yes. That is basically a signal-to-noise experiment on yourself.

If you reduce background stimulation first—less novelty, fewer cues, calmer baseline—then selectively reintroduce one thing at a time, changes in your state become easier to notice:

low baseline
→ introduce cue
→ observe body / attention / emotion
→ remove cue
→ repeat
→ compare delta

That can help answer:

“What actually affects me?”

It is much less reliable for answering:

“What is another person intentionally doing to me?”

Because the same arousal or emotional shift could come from your own associations, expectation, timing, memory, physiology, or the environment. Your state is evidence about your response, not direct proof of someone else’s intent.

So the clean split is:

internal delta → evidence of sensitivity
external intent → separate hypothesis

That makes the method useful without letting it turn into mind-reading.

In Pantheon language, I’d call it Selective Receptivity Testing:

quiet the field → admit one signal → measure delta → reset → compare

Very similar to experimental isolation in code or science: if everything is firing at once, you cannot tell which input caused what.